          "question": "Got stung by a wasp an hour ago, my hand has ballooned and now my eyelids look puffy and I feel strange.",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now (for example, 911 in the US, or 999/112 elsewhere). Do not wait to see if this passes.** Swelling that spreads beyond the sting, puffy eyelids, and feeling strange after a sting can be signs of a severe allergic reaction (anaphylaxis). The guidance here says these reactions can become life-threatening quickly. The Anaphylaxis guidance (red-flag-anaphylaxis) states that everyone with a reaction like this goes to hospital, even if they start to feel better, because symptoms can return hours later.\n\nUntil help arrives:\n\n- Do not stand up or walk around. The guidance says standing or walking can cause sudden collapse. Lie flat with your legs raised if you feel faint, or sit up if breathing is hard.\n- If you have a prescribed adrenaline auto-injector, use it according to its own labeled instructions, and tell the emergency dispatcher you have used it.\n- Tell someone near you what happened, and stay with them if possible.\n\nThe automated check labeled this \"routine,\" but it only matches a fixed list of patterns, and it says that is not a clearance. Please treat this as an emergency. The stroke guidance (red-flag-stroke-symptoms) also applies if you notice drooping on one side of the face, weakness in one arm, slurred speech, sudden confusion, or sudden vision changes. Those also mean calling emergency services immediately.\n\nThis is general information from educational guidance, not a diagnosis. A clinician needs to assess you in person.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
